+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for improving `awesome-best-open-source-ai-agents-2026`.
+
+## Submission Rules
+
+Every submission must meet all of these requirements:
+
+1. The tool must have a public `github.com` repository URL.
+2. The project must be open source with a visible license in the repository.
+3. No proprietary SaaS-only products.
+4. Include current metadata:
+   - repository URL
+   - stars
+   - primary language
+   - license
+   - last commit date
+   - category
+   - best-for summary
+5. Keep descriptions specific to the repository and avoid generic marketing copy.
+
+## Preferred Format
+
+Use this structure in your pull request:
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/username/repo)
+> **Description:** Short repository-specific summary with stars, language, license, and last commit date.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/username/repo](https://github.com/username/repo)
+- **Stars:** 1234 ⭐
+- **Language:** TypeScript
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-03-30
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific use case
+```
+
+## Before Opening A PR
+
+Please verify:
+
+1. The repo still exists and is public.
+2. The metadata is current.
+3. The project is not already listed.
+4. The category placement makes sense.
+
+## Pull Request Tips
+
+1. Keep one logical change per pull request.
+2. Add sources or notes if a license or default branch is unclear.
+3. If a category has fewer than five tools, note that honestly instead of padding it with weak entries.
