+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI agents for coding in 2026?
+For coding-heavy work, start with `gemini-cli`, `goose`, `open-swe`, `plandex`, and `qwen-code`. They are the most directly aligned with repository navigation, code editing, terminal workflows, and developer automation.
+
+### Which open source AI agents can I self-host?
+The strongest self-hosted options in this list are `LocalAI`, `LibreChat`, `DB-GPT`, `Khoj`, and `OpenViking`. They are useful when privacy, internal data access, or infrastructure control matters more than SaaS convenience.
+
+### Are all tools in this awesome list real GitHub repositories?
+Yes. Every entry links to a public `github.com` repository. SaaS-only products, closed demos, and tools without a public GitHub repo were excluded by design.
+
+### Which open source AI agents are best for browser automation?
+For browser-first use cases, check `UI-TARS-desktop`, `nanobrowser`, `browser-agent`, `steel-browser`, and `browserable`. These repos are the closest matches for computer-use and web interaction workflows.
+
+### What if I want an open source AI agent framework instead of a ready-made app?
+Choose `adk-python`, `adk-go`, `voltagent`, `ten-framework`, or `agentset`. These are better when you want to build your own product, internal tool, or workflow rather than deploy a finished assistant.
+
+### Why are some famous AI tools missing from this list?
+This repository only includes public GitHub repositories and excludes products that are primarily proprietary SaaS. If a tool did not have a verifiable public repo URL or did not fit the open source requirement, it was left out.
+
